<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · 1404 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Yigdal</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">יִגְדַּל</bdi> / <bdi lang="he-Latn">Yigdal</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Jewish hymn that puts the thirteen principles of faith into verse, sung at the start and end of synagogue services.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>disputed: Daniel ben Judah or Immanuel the Roman</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional; many tunes</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Jewish communities, Ashkenazi and Sephardic</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>completed in 1404, by one scholarly account</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (medieval Hebrew hymn; the author is disputed)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yigdal">Yigdal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> for &quot;be exalted&quot;, is a Jewish hymn that sets in verse the 13 principles of faith formulated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a>. In various rites it shares with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a> the place of honour at the opening of the morning service and the close of the evening service. It was not the only verse setting of the 13 principles, but it has outlived all the others, in Hebrew or in other languages.</p><p>Who wrote it is debated. Leopold Zunz held that it was the work of Daniel ben <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, who spent eight years improving it before completing it in 1404. Some read a signature in its last line naming a Yechiel son of Baruch, whose identity is uncertain. Hartwig Hirschfeld argued for the poet Immanuel the Roman, who wrote several poems on the 13 principles, among them a 72-line &quot;Poem Based on the 13 Articles&quot; that shares <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yigdal">Yigdal</a>'s rhythm, rhyme and phrasing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi Jews</a> sing thirteen lines, one for each principle, and repeat the last, on the resurrection of the dead, when the cantor and congregation sing it in alternation; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a> sing it together throughout and add a fourteenth line.</p><p>It has more traditional tunes than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a>, and older ones. In the Sephardic rite of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English communities it is often sung to a melody that stands for the day. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, for two centuries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> congregations have had a fixed tune for each occasion, all sung in alternation by the cantor and congregation. The best known, the Friday evening tune, may date from the 17th century or earlier and was also used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>; a tune for the evenings of Rosh Hashana and Yom Kippur was probably the work of a Polish cantor of the early 17th century and spread westward; three tunes for the pilgrimage festivals go back at least to the early 18th century, and the choral response of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> tune took its final shape from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Mombach. Isaac Nathan used the lively Sukkot tune in 1815 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a>'s &quot;The Wild Gazelle&quot;, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> Melodies.</p><p>Not everyone sings it. Most Hasidic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> leave it out, because Isaac Luria reportedly omitted it from his prayer book, rejecting poems later than the Talmudic era as not kabbalistic enough, though following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> Horowitz most treat it as a sacred hymn. Syrian Jews sing it before Kiddush on Friday night rather than at the end of services, and Rabbi Joseph B. Soloveitchik opposed reciting it at the end of prayers as an imitation of the Christian custom of a closing catechism.</p><p>The hymn also passed into Christian worship. The Methodist Thomas Olivers heard the cantor Myer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, who also sang on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> opera stage as Michael Leoni, sing it at the Great Synagogue of London, and around 1770 wrote &quot;The God of Abraham Praise&quot;, a paraphrase set to the tune now called &quot;Leoni&quot;; John Wesley's hymnbook printed it, much Christianised, in 1785. In the late 19th century Rabbi Max Landsberg and the Unitarian minister Newton M. Mann made a closer translation, &quot;Praise to the Living God&quot;, first printed in the Reform Jewish Union Hymnal and since used in many Christian hymnals.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A creed in verse: line by line it states the principles of Jewish faith set out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a>, one to a line, ending with the resurrection of the dead. Its mood is one of solemn praise, though some of its tunes are festive.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hebrew</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hebrew</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">יִגְדַּל אֱלֹהִים חַי וְיִשְׁתַּבַּח</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">yigdal elohim khai veyishtabbakh</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Exalted be the living God and praised;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">נִמְצָא וְאֵין עֵת אֶל מְצִיאוּתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">nimtsa veen et el metsiuto</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he exists, and there is no time bounding his existence.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">אֶחָד וְאֵין יָחִיד כְּיִחוּדוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">ekhad veen yakhid keyikhudo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One, and there is no oneness like his oneness;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">נֶעְלָם וְגַם אֵין סוֹף לְאַחְדוּתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">nelam vegam en sof leakhduto</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>hidden, and there is also no end to his unity.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">אֵין לוֹ דְמוּת הַגּוּף וְאֵינוֹ גוּף</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">en lo demut haguf veeno guf</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He has no likeness of a body, and he is not a body;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לֹא נַעֲרֹךְ אֵלָיו קְדֻשָּׁתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">lo naarokh elav qedushato</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we cannot set his holiness beside him [?].</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">קַדְמוֹן לְכָל דָּבָר אֲשֶׁר נִבְרָא</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">qadmon lekhol davar asher nivra</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Prior to every thing that was created,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">רִאשׁוֹן וְאֵין רֵאשִׁית לְרֵאשִׁיתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">rishon veen reshit lereshito</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>first, and there is no beginning to his beginning.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">הִנּוֹ אֲדוֹן עוֹלָם לְכָל נוֹצָר</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">hinno adon olam lekhol notsar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Behold, he is Lord of the world to every creature;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">יוֹרֶה גְדֻלָּתוֹ וּמַלְכוּתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">yoreh gedullato umalkhuto</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he shows his greatness and his kingship.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">שֶׁפַע נְבוּאָתוֹ נְתָנוֹ, אֶל</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">shefa nevuato netano, el</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The flow of his prophecy he gave to</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">אַנְשֵׁי סְגֻלָּתוֹ וְתִפְאַרְתּוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">anshe segullato vetifarto</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the men of his treasured portion and his glory.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לֹא קָם בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל כְּמשֶׁה עוֹד</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">lo qam beyisrael kemosheh od</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There has not arisen again in Israel one like Moses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">נָבִיא וּמַבִּיט אֶת תְּמוּנָתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">navi umabbit et temunato</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a prophet who beholds his likeness.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">תּוֹרַת אֱמֶת נָתַן לְעַמּוֹ אֵל</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">torat emet natan leammo el</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A Torah of truth God gave to his people</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">עַל יַד נְבִיאוֹ נֶאֱמַן בֵּיתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">al yad nevio neeman beto</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>by the hand of his prophet, the trusted one of his house.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לֹא יַחֲלִיף הָאֵל וְלֹא יָמִיר</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">lo yakhalif hael velo yamir</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God will not exchange nor alter</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">דָּתוֹ, לְעוֹלָמִים לְזוּלָתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">dato, leolamim lezulato</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>his law, for all ages, for any other.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">צוֹפֶה וְיוֹדֵעַ סְתָרֵינוּ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">tsofeh veyodea setarenu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He watches and knows our secrets;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מַבִּיט לְסוֹף דָּבָר בְּקַדְמוּתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">mabbit lesof davar beqadmuto</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he sees the end of a thing at its beginning.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">גּוֹמֵל לְאִישׁ חֶסֶד כְּמִפְעָלוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">gomel leish khesed kemifalo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He repays a man with kindness according to his deed;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">נוֹתֵן לְרָשָׁע רָע כְּרִשְׁעָתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">noten lerasha ra kerishato</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he gives the wicked evil according to his wickedness.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">יִשְׁלַח לְקֵץ יָמִין מְשִׁיחֵנוּ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">yishlakh leqets yamin meshikhenu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He will send at the end of days our anointed one</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לִפְדּוֹת מְחַכֵּי קֵץ יְשׁוּעָתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">lifdot mekhakke qets yeshuato</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to redeem those who wait for the end, his salvation.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מֵתִים יְחַיֶּה אֵל בְּרֹב חַסְדּוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">metim yekhayyeh el berov khasdo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The dead God will bring to life in his great kindness;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בָּרוּךְ עֲדֵי עַד שֵׁם תְּהִלָּתוֹ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">barukh ade ad shem tehillato</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>blessed for ever and ever be the name of his praise.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Medieval Hebrew hymn, its author disputed. Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%99%D7%92%D7%93%D7%9C" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (SBL general-purpose).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>The hymn sets out the Thirteen Principles of Maimonides, one or two to a line.</li><li>line 3.2: 'lo naarokh elav qedushato' is read as 'we cannot compare his holiness to anything' or 'we cannot describe his holiness'; the construction is compressed.</li><li>line 12.1: 'yamin' here is the poetic form of 'yamim', 'days', not 'right hand'.</li><li>line 12.2: 'qets yeshuato': 'the end (the appointed time) of his salvation'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="t3pI8u2vdRw" data-title="Yigdal: Cantor Netanel Hershtik and the Hampton Synagogue Choir"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Cantor Netanel Hershtik and the Hampton Synagogue Choir</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Hampton Synagogue. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t3pI8u2vdRw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The God of Abraham Praise (Thomas Olivers)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1770</span><span class="sg-chain-h">paraphrase, to the synagogue tune &quot;Leoni&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Wild Gazelle (Isaac Nathan, words by Lord Byron, Hebrew Melodies)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1815</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (the Sukkot tune)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Praise to the Living God (Max Landsberg and Newton M. Mann)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">late 19th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The 13 principles of faith (Maimonides)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the hymn sets them in verse</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Poem Based on the 13 Articles (Immanuel the Roman)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">shares its rhythm, rhyme and phrasing, by Hartwig Hirschfeld's argument</span></li></ul></section>
